WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.000
OpenAI's product lineup finally has a simple mental model that actually makes sense. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, episode 132. A recent post on the ChatGPT subreddit lays out OpenAI's tools in three clear categories that finally feel logical. ChatGPT works like a smart friend you text back and forth with for questions or brainstorming. You type something, it replies, and the whole conversation stays inside that one window.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:43.180
Codex takes the next step and acts like a coworker who sits at an actual desk with files and tools right in front of them. It can read documents, run commands, and edit things because it has its own computer-like space to work in.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:52.200
Work is different again because it functions like a remote assistant who can reach into your email, documents, and apps without needing to sit at your desk.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:01:02.700
The key difference shows up when you only have your phone handy. Codex needs a full computer nearby, while work connects through the cloud so you can hand it a task from anywhere.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:13.740
Think of it like ordering food three different ways. One is texting a friend for a recipe suggestion, another is asking a cook who has their own kitchen to prepare the meal on site.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:20.800
The third is calling a delivery service that pulls ingredients from multiple stores and brings the finished dish to you.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:29.660
This split matters for students or anyone juggling school and part-time jobs because you can pick the right helper based on what device you have in your hand.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:38.260
Many people feel overwhelmed when companies release several similar tools, yet this simple breakdown shows exactly when to reach for each one.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:46.320
The post points out that work can also run on desktop, but the mobile first view is what finally made the lineup feel coherent.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:52.980
You can read the full explanation yourself on the chat GPT subreddit and see the real examples the poster shared.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:02:02.300
The writer is just a regular user, so the language stays practical and skips marketing talk. You know how your phone suggests the next word while you are texting.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:09.960
Now if picture that same idea except the AI can also reach out and use other apps or files the way a helpful assistant would.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:18.700
First it reads your full request in one go, then it decides which tool or document it needs like checking a calendar or pulling up an email.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:25.760
After that it takes the action quietly in the background and returns a finished result instead of stopping at advice.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:32.780
A regular chatbot ends at suggestions while an agent actually carries out the steps across different services.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.320
That separation becomes clear when you line up the three open AI tools side by side.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.520
Chat GPT stays inside the conversation window the whole time.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:47.580
CodeX requires a real computer environment with local files and terminal access.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:52.720
Work skips the local computer entirely and works through cloud connections instead.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:03:02.540
The result is that you can ask work to find relevant emails, check documents, scan a slack discussion and create a summary even when you only have your phone.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:09.880
One tool talks the other does. That difference is what separates simple chat from the newer agent style tools.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:16.740
Safari offers an AI that can search and explain ancient Jewish writing stretching back 3000 years.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.800
It is useful if you are curious about history, religion, or how AI handles very old language.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:28.580
Anyone can try it by visiting the Safari website and typing a question about a text or topic.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:36.380
Start with something simple like asking what the Torah says about kindness and notice how the AI pulls answers from its huge collection.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:43.280
The tool works on both specific passages and broader themes so you can experiment with either style of question.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:48.560
Clawed can now run in your Chrome sidebar right next to any web page you are viewing.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:53.340
This means you do not have to switch tabs when you want help while researching or writing.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:59.240
If you already use Clawed, add the browser extension and open the sidebar to chat while you browse.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:06.500
The sidebar becomes a live workspace where the model can see the current page and answer questions about it without leaving the tab.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.320
This works on school research pages, creative writing documents, or any other site you have open.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:20.700
Google's Pixel Buds are gaining deeper Gemini integration so voice commands handle music, directions, and quick questions more smoothly.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:26.000
The update lets the earbuds manage more complex requests without needing your phone screen.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:32.520
Amazon updated its rules so Twitch streamers can choose whether their videos help train future AI models.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:41.860
The opt out covers streams, videos on demand, clips, chat messages, and channel images or text, and it applies only to future training.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:47.860
That is it for today. Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.700
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:57.580
That is literally how learning works. Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we will see you tomorrow.

